(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cutaneous-bacterial-infections-vignettes"]={"title":"Cutaneous Bacterial Infections Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 15-year-old boy with untreated acne develops tender fluctuant nodules with purulent drainage over the same areas. Which best describes what has happened?","choices":["A primary bacterial infection has arisen in previously normal and intact skin","The acne has progressed to its nodular form without any additional infection","A foreign body reaction to hair has developed within the affected follicles","A secondary bacterial infection has developed within skin already affected by acne"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — secondary infection arises in skin damaged by an existing condition. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with type 2 diabetes has asymptomatic red-brown patches on the inner thighs and between the fourth and fifth toes. Under a Wood's lamp they fluoresce coral-red. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Inverse psoriasis","Erythrasma","Tinea cruris","Cutaneous candidiasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — coral-red fluorescence in intertriginous skin, with diabetics at high risk. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man who injects drugs has a tender fluctuant swelling of the forearm that developed at an injection site. Which best describes how this lesion arose?","choices":["Obstruction of an apocrine duct followed by rupture into the surrounding dermal tissue","Spread of streptococci along the superficial lymphatics of the upper dermal layer","Traumatic inoculation of bacteria into the skin, which is how an abscess differs from a furuncle","Infection of a hair follicle spreading into the adjacent subcutaneous tissue of the arm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a furuncle arises from an infected hair follicle instead. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 27-year-old Black man has tender erythematous papules along the jawline and neck, several with a visible hair shaft at the centre. They appeared after he began shaving daily with a five-blade razor. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea barbae from a dermatophyte infection","Pseudofolliculitis barbae","Bacterial folliculitis of the beard area","Acne vulgaris affecting the jaw and neck"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a foreign body reaction to cut hair re-entering the skin in a shaved area. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with alcohol use disorder has severe pain in the thigh that is far worse than the modest erythema on examination. He was diagnosed with cellulitis two days ago and is no better on oral antibiotics. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Progressing cellulitis needing intravenous therapy","Deep vein thrombosis of the femoral vein","Compartment syndrome from an unnoticed injury","Necrotizing fasciitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — pain out of proportion to examination, and no response to antibiotics at forty-eight hours. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with suspected necrotizing fasciitis of the thigh has a computed tomography scan showing gas in the soft tissue fascial planes. Which organism does this suggest?","choices":["Clostridium perfringens, since gas is not present with Group A streptococcus","Group A streptococcus, since gas is not present with Clostridium perfringens","Staphylococcus aureus, which produces gas through epidermolytic toxins","Pseudomonas aeruginosa, which produces gas in inadequately perfused tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ultrasound also demonstrates air bubbles in soft tissue. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 129"},{"q":"A 39-year-old dishwasher is diagnosed with chronic paronychia. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["A ten-day course of oral cephalexin covering staphylococci and streptococci","Topical mupirocin to the nail folds until the swelling has fully resolved","Keep the hands as dry as possible and use a broad-spectrum topical antifungal","Warm soaks three times daily and incision if any purulent material collects"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with oral fluconazole reserved for severe cases. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has early acute paronychia with erythema and tenderness but no fluctuance. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["A broad-spectrum topical antifungal applied twice daily","Immediate removal of the nail plate under local block","Warm water soaks for twenty minutes three times daily","Incision and drainage under digital block anaesthesia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Incision and drainage is reserved for severe cases with purulent collection. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with a prosthetic heart valve has a carbuncle requiring drainage. Which additional measure is specifically indicated?","choices":["Endocarditis prophylaxis before the procedure","Nasal mupirocin for five days before the procedure","Blood cultures repeated daily for three days after","Deferral of drainage until an antibiotic has taken effect"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is named for at-risk patients in both furuncle and carbuncle management. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman who had a mastectomy with axillary node clearance has recurrent erysipelas of that arm. Which factor most explains the recurrence?","choices":["Traumatic inoculation of bacteria into the dermis","Obstruction of the apocrine ducts in the axilla","Impaired lymphatic drainage from the node clearance","Nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus after surgery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is the first risk factor named for erysipelas. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man is admitted with necrotizing fasciitis after dental sepsis. Which referrals should be arranged?","choices":["Wound care nursing alone, with review by the general team each day","A team approach with surgical intensive care, ideally at a burn or trauma centre","Dermatology alone, since the presenting problem is a skin infection","Infectious diseases alone, since antibiotic selection determines outcome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — consultations are described as necessary rather than optional. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 131"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man is being treated for erysipelas of the leg. Which is the most appropriate supportive management alongside the antibiotic?","choices":["Compression bandaging applied from the toes to the knee for the first week","Dilute acetic acid compresses applied for twenty minutes several times daily","Symptomatic treatment of aches and fever, hydration, cold compresses and elevation of the limb","Warm soaks three times daily with incision if any purulent material has collected"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — prompt treatment matters because progression can be rapid. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman is starting oral isotretinoin for nodular acne. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Contraception can be stopped as soon as the treatment course has been completed in full","Blood donation is encouraged during treatment because the drug is cleared through the plasma","Pregnancy testing is required before, monthly during and five weeks after treatment, with two forms of contraception preferred","Pregnancy testing is required only before starting, after which monthly liver function tests replace it entirely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with iPledge enrolment, a one-month supply at a time, and no blood donation. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl has widespread inflammatory papules and pustules across the face, chest and back that have not improved after eight weeks of topical benzoyl peroxide with a retinoid. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Add an oral tetracycline, keeping benzoyl peroxide to limit resistance","Add topical salicylic acid as a second comedolytic to the current regimen","Stop all topical therapy and observe for a further eight to twelve weeks","Refer immediately for intralesional steroid injection of the worst lesions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — inadequate response over six to eight weeks is the stated trigger. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has hot tub folliculitis over the trunk. He is systemically well and the rash began four days ago. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Oral ciprofloxacin for a ten-day course to eradicate the organism completely","Incision and drainage of the largest pustules with culture of the material","Topical mupirocin three times daily until every lesion has fully resolved","Reassurance with dilute acetic acid compresses, since most clear in two to ten days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — 3 tablespoons of 5% vinegar in a pint of water, 20 minutes, two to four times daily. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has had furuncles recur repeatedly over two years. He has a body mass index of 36 and a haemoglobin A1c of 8.4%. Which three factors should be addressed?","choices":["Occupational water immersion, chemical contact and nail biting","Obesity, diabetes and nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus","Smoking, hot weather and obstruction of the apocrine ducts","Impaired lymphatic drainage, tinea pedis and previous impetigo"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — those are the three predisposing factors named for recurrent furunculosis. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has a drained furuncle growing methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. He asks why the first antibiotic did not work. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["The organism produces epidermolytic toxins that inactivate oral antibiotics","The empiric agents cephalexin and dicloxacillin do not cover this resistant organism","The organism was not reached because the lesion had not yet been drained at all","The antibiotic was taken for too short a period to eradicate any skin infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the culture is what redirects therapy to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, clindamycin or doxycycline. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with pseudofolliculitis barbae is prescribed topical tretinoin. Which is the most appropriate explanation of what it does?","choices":["It relieves the hyperkeratosis, removing the epidermis the emerging hair embeds in","It kills the Staphylococcus aureus colonising the shaved skin of the beard area","It permanently destroys the follicles so that the hairs will not grow back at all","It softens the hair shaft so that each hair curls away from the skin as it grows"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mild corticosteroids reduce inflammation and topical antibiotics reduce colonisation. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has extensive hidradenitis suppurativa in both axillae that has failed medical therapy for years. She asks whether anything can cure it. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Laser hair removal of the axillae gives the best chance of permanent cure","Spironolactone taken indefinitely gives the best chance of permanent cure","Wide excision of the affected areas gives the best chance of permanent cure","Long-term systemic antibiotic therapy gives the best chance of permanent cure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Large fluctuant cysts are meanwhile incised and drained. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 18-year-old man with severe nodular acne has failed a topical retinoid with an oral antibiotic and benzoyl peroxide. Which test is required before the next treatment is started?","choices":["A pregnancy test is not needed for him, but iPledge enrolment of the prescriber is required","A bacterial culture of a nodule, to confirm that Cutibacterium acnes is still present","A fasting insulin and lipid panel, to identify the insulin resistance driving the acne","A skin biopsy of a nodular lesion, to exclude an alternative inflammatory disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — isotretinoin requires prescriber enrolment and a one-month supply at a time. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy has impetigo covering much of one arm and both cheeks, too extensive for topical therapy. He has no drug allergies. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral fluconazole, which covers candidal superinfection","Oral cephalexin, the drug of choice in children","Oral doxycycline, which covers both likely organisms","Oral ciprofloxacin, which covers resistant organisms"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is used where there is penicillin allergy. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 4-year-old girl has three crusted impetigo lesions on one forearm. She is systemically well. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Warm compresses alone, since the condition is self-limiting and resolves without treatment","Mupirocin ointment after removing the crusts, since topical therapy suits limited lesions","Oral cephalexin, since impetigo in children always requires systemic antibiotic treatment","Oral doxycycline, since it covers both staphylococcal and streptococcal skin disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is as effective as oral therapy with fewer side effects. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man whose two children developed hot tub folliculitis last month asks how to stop it happening again. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Shower thoroughly within thirty minutes of leaving the water on every occasion","Raise the water temperature above forty degrees to kill the responsible organism","Limit each session to twenty minutes and avoid submerging the trunk entirely","Maintain continuous water filtration, monitor disinfectant levels and change the water often"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Showering after contact does not prevent the infection. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 30-year-old primary school teacher has impetigo across one forearm. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Obtain a culture, because her occupation places her at high risk for resistant organisms","Treat empirically with mupirocin, because culture is never indicated in adult impetigo","Treat empirically with oral doxycycline, because adult impetigo is usually staphylococcal","Defer treatment until swabs of every household contact have been taken and reported"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a health-care worker or teacher is the stated example of that risk. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"The parent of a 6-year-old with impetigo asks when he can go back to school. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["He may return once every crusted lesion has completely healed and the skin is clear","He may return immediately, since impetigo does not spread from one person to another","He may return after ten full days from the appearance of the very first lesion","He may return once treatment has been underway for twenty-four to forty-eight hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and towels, clothing, bath water, washcloths and razors must not be shared. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with acne asks how soon she should expect to see a difference. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Improvement takes one to two weeks, and all affected sites respond at the same rate","Improvement takes six to eight months, and the face is the slowest site to respond","Improvement is immediate, and any delay indicates the regimen should be changed","Improvement takes four to six weeks, and the back and chest may take three to four months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clinical improvement is judged by new lesion count at six to eight weeks. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 47-year-old healthy woman has a small area of cellulitis on the forearm with minimal pain, no fever and no risk factors for serious illness. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic approach?","choices":["Venous duplex ultrasound, since deep vein thrombosis must be excluded first","No workup, since the diagnosis is clinical in limited disease without systemic signs","Blood cultures and a punch biopsy, since organism identification guides therapy","Plain films of the forearm, since underlying osteomyelitis must be excluded"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Serious infection warrants blood cultures, punch biopsy, blood count and creatine phosphokinase. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man who bites his nails has acute paronychia requiring an oral antibiotic. Which agent is specifically suggested for his exposure?","choices":["Clindamycin, because of exposure to oral flora","Ciprofloxacin, because of exposure to oral flora","Doxycycline, because of exposure to oral flora","Penicillin V, because of exposure to oral flora"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Amoxicillin-clavulanate and cephalexin are the other named options. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with erysipelas of the leg has a clinically classic presentation. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic approach?","choices":["No routine testing, since blood and tissue cultures have extremely low yield","Blood cultures in every case, since organism identification directs the antibiotic","Computed tomography of the limb to define the depth of tissue involvement","Punch biopsy of the leading edge to separate this from contact dermatitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Leukocytosis and raised inflammatory markers are common but not diagnostic. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has had folliculitis recur four times in a year despite topical treatment each time. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin a three-month course of oral doxycycline with topical benzoyl peroxide","Refer for laser hair removal to eliminate the follicles permanently in that area","Reassure that recurrence is expected and continue the same topical treatment","Swab the nose for Staphylococcus aureus carriage and treat with mupirocin if positive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — nasal mupirocin twice daily for five days addresses the carrier state. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 46"}]};
